# <project-name> — web-pwa

<!-- Audit: B.9.2 (b9-2-web-pwa) -->

The installable **Qwik City PWA** surface of `<project-name>`, the default channel
for Web, Android and desktop. On iOS, when push delivery is critical, the native
Flutter `app` surface at the repository root is the prescribed fallback
(`docs/ARCHITECTURE-TARGET.md` §6.3).

Governed by `.forge/standards/web-frontend.yaml` (framework choice) and
`.forge/standards/pwa.yaml` (installability, offline behaviour, push delivery).

## Commands

```bash
npm install
npm run dev        # vite --mode ssr
npm run build      # qwik build
npm run preview    # production preview (required to exercise the Service Worker)
```

## ⚠️ PITFALL — do not upgrade Vite past 7.x

`vite` is pinned **exactly**. Vite 8.x is the npm `latest` (8.1.5 at 2026-07-28) but
is **excluded** by `@builder.io/qwik` `peerDependencies` `">=5 <8"`, re-confirmed
live on 2026-07-28. Installing the default `vite` yields an incompatible peer and a
broken build. Re-check the peer range before changing this pin.

## ⚠️ Push notifications do not work out of the box — this is by design

This archetype is `layer_profile: client-only`: it has **no backend layer**. The
scaffold therefore ships only the **client half** of Web Push — subscription and
notification display. It contains no push server, no VAPID key generation and no
key storage, because all three are backend concerns (ADR-B9-2-005).

To make push actually deliver you must:

1. Generate a VAPID key pair yourself, and keep the private key **out of this repo**.
2. Expose the public key to the build as `PUBLIC_VAPID_KEY`.
3. Run your own sender that receives the `PushSubscription` from
   `src/lib/push.ts` and pushes to it.

Until you do, `subscribeToPush()` succeeds and no notification ever arrives.

## Offline behaviour

`src/routes/service-worker.ts` precaches `/offline/` on install and serves it for
any failed navigation, so the app never shows a browser network-error page. Keep
the offline route dependency-free: anything it imports must be precached too, or
the shell breaks exactly when it is needed.

## No backend client here

Unlike the flagship `frontend/web-public/` surface, this one ships **no**
`@connectrpc/*` dependency and no `src/lib/connect-client.ts`. There is nothing to
call. If a future change gives this archetype a backend, adding a Connect client is
purely additive.

## Sign-in — and the four things this scaffold deliberately does not do

`src/lib/auth/` is a browser OIDC client: authorization code flow with PKCE (S256),
`state` and `nonce` from the Web Crypto CSPRNG, discovery from the issuer, and full
ID-token validation on the way back. It is built on [`oauth4webapi`][o4wa] — zero
dependencies, WebCrypto only.

Configuration lives in `../oidc-provider.json`, at the project root, shared with the
Flutter app: the `issuer` and `scopes` are declared once and each surface carries its
own `clientId` and `redirectUri`. Replace the `TODO_REPLACE_*` values before signing
in; the client refuses to start a flow while they are still there.

[o4wa]: https://github.com/panva/oauth4webapi

### 1. The two surfaces hold independent sessions — this is not SSO

The native app and this PWA are **separate clients with separate sessions**. Signing
in on one does **not** sign you in on the other: a token minted by the app is held in
the app, and nothing transports it here. They share an identity *provider*, not a
session.

In practice the second sign-in is often invisible — the provider still has its own
cookie and returns immediately without a prompt — which is exactly what makes this
worth stating. It looks like single sign-on right up until the provider session is
gone, and then it is plainly not.

If you need real cross-surface SSO, that is a provider-side and BFF-side design
decision, not something a scaffold can grant.

### 2. A reload ends the session

Tokens live in a module variable in `src/lib/auth/session.ts` and **nowhere else**.
Reload the page and the session is gone.

That is deliberate. The native surface has Keychain and encrypted SharedPreferences;
the browser has no equivalent. `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` are readable by
any script running in this origin, so one XSS or one bad transitive dependency turns
a stored refresh token into a durable credential an attacker can use offline, long
after they have lost access to the page. This scaffold will not write one there.

What a reload actually costs: `login()` again — a full redirect to the provider and
back. **As long as the provider's own session cookie is still valid** that round-trip
normally completes without showing the user anything, so it reads as a flicker rather
than a login form. When that cookie is gone, they sign in properly.

**No silent re-authorization is implemented.** `prompt=none` — the non-interactive
check that renews a session in a hidden iframe without a visible navigation — is
**not** wired up here: `beginAuthorization()` sends no `prompt` parameter, and nothing
attempts a session restore on boot. The outcome above comes from an ordinary
authorization request meeting a live provider cookie, not from a silent flow. If you
want a true silent renewal, adding `prompt=none` plus `login_required` error handling
is the thing to build, and it is a real feature rather than a configuration change.

What to build if that is not enough: a **BFF** (backend-for-frontend) that holds the
tokens server-side and gives the browser an `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` cookie.
That is the standard answer, and it is a *backend* — outside a `client-only`
archetype. Adding one is a deliberate architectural step, not a setting.

The one thing that does touch browser storage is the in-flight transaction
(`state`, `nonce`, `code_verifier`) in `sessionStorage`, because signing in is a
full-page navigation and the document that minted those values is destroyed before
the callback runs. They are single-use, tab-scoped, deleted the moment they are read,
and useless without the authorization code. No token is ever written there.

### 3. Compliance tier T3 — the self-host obligation is yours

`.forge/standards/identity.yaml` is tier-aware and states that **T3 requires a
self-hosted identity provider**. This archetype is `client-only`: it has no infra
layer and can self-host nothing.

The obligation does not disappear — it moves. If you operate at T3, the provider you
point `oidc-provider.json` at must itself satisfy the self-host requirement, deployed
and operated separately from this repository. The standard is correct; it is simply
scoped to archetypes that can deploy a provider, and this one cannot.

### 4. No client secret, ever

Both surfaces are **public clients**. There is no secret to configure and none is
shipped — a secret in a mobile binary or a JS bundle is readable by anyone who has
either. PKCE is what protects the code exchange.
